course: dictionary
lesson: 5228
title: A word from old clothes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A jerkin is a short jacket with no sleeves. Long ago, men wore them over their shirts, often made of leather. Today a jerkin can also be a bright work jacket that workers wear so drivers can see them.
explain: The word is a noun, so you can say a jerkin or some jerkins. The writer of a play used a jerkin to show what a character wore. In «meadow-sweet ... in amber jerkin», the flower is dressed like a person from an old story.
explain: When you meet a costume word in a story, look at the time and place around it. Words like olden days, soldier, woodman or castle tell you the clothes are old-fashioned. Then use a picture in your head to check your meaning.
fact: A jerkin is a short, close-fitting jacket without sleeves.
fact: Jerkins were often made of leather and worn by men in the 1500s and 1600s.
fact: Today a jerkin can be a sleeveless work jacket, often bright so people can see the wearer.
fact: Context clues help: old-fashioned settings, such as «the ancient castle», tell you the clothes are from long ago.
fact: «Diffident» means shy, and «jerkin» means an old short jacket.
exercise: In «meadow-sweet ... in amber jerkin», what is a jerkin? | A tall hat | A pair of boots | A long dress with a hood | *A short jacket without sleeves
exercise: Men in history lessons often wore a leather jerkin. In which times were they worn? | The year 2050 | Only by children at school | The 1900s only | *The 1500s and 1600s
exercise: In «the diffident and delaying dog-rose», what does diffident mean? | Bright green | Very hungry | Loud and bold | *Shy
test: Road workers wear a bright sleeveless jacket so drivers can see them. What is it called? | A pair of rubber boots | *A high-visibility jerkin | A woollen hat | A velvet cloak
test: A woodman in a story wears a leather jerkin with no sleeves. What does jerkin mean? | A kind of tent | A long, hooded cloak | *A short, sleeveless jacket | A type of shoe
exam: Which sentence shows that a jerkin is an old-fashioned piece of clothing? | A jerkin is a long, hooded cloak for rain | Jerkins are always made of paper | Pupils wear a jerkin to the swimming pool | *Soldiers in the olden days wore a leather jerkin over their shirts
